; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The epoch inventory: what a deployment leaves beside a closed run of packs, and the only artifact of an
; epoch a reviewer can be handed alone.
;
; A deployment retires receipts out of its live store on the bounds it configures, and an evidence epoch is
; what survives that: a directory holding one sealed pack and one retention artifact per closed window, and
; one document at its root stating what the run covers, what each pack in it says, and what the run adds up
; to. This file states that document, and it states it in the public repository because the estate's claim is
; that a reviewer holding an epoch inventory can check it with published code and no service of ours in the
; loop. The generator that fills the layout runs on the paid side; the layout, its reader and the arithmetic
; a reader owes itself are here.
;
; `packages/receipt/src/epoch-inventory.ts` reads this layout and writes it, and refuses each shape it will
; not accept by code. `packages/receipt/schemas/epoch-inventory-v1.schema.json` is its machine-readable twin,
; `docs/epoch-inventory-v1.md` is the prose a reimplementer opens first, and
; `packages/receipt/test/epoch-inventory.test.ts` holds the blocks this file declares, that twin and the
; reader's own member lists against each other, so no one of the three can widen alone. Where a sentence here
; and those files disagree about a member, the reader is what a client actually runs and this file is wrong.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory = #6.18(COSE_Sign1-Epoch-Inventory-COSE)

; The envelope every signed document in this estate uses, unchanged but for the content type: COSE_Sign1 per
; RFC 9052 section 4.2, encoded per RFC 8949 with Core Deterministic Encoding, so a reader rebuilds the bytes
; that were signed rather than guessing which of several equivalent spellings was used.
;
; The payload carries no `.cbor`, and that is a statement and not an omission. It is the JSON document a
; deployment writes into the epoch directory, as UTF-8, and it is signed byte for byte: no re-encode, no key
; order chosen here, no whitespace decision. A deployment that reformats its own inventory has to reseal it,
; which is what `sealDeploymentManifest` does for the document at `GET /deployment-manifest` and for the same
; reason. The alternative was to put the run's figures in CBOR, which would have made the signed artifact a
; different document from the file an operator leaves on a volume and handed over, and the point of this
; container is that the file a reviewer holds is the file that was signed.
COSE_Sign1-Epoch-Inventory-COSE = [
    protected: bstr .cbor Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Protected-Header,
    unprotected: { * any => any }, ; the map that carries no claim; see the note below the document
    payload: bstr,                 ; the epoch inventory document, as written into the epoch directory
    signature: bstr .size 64
]

; The same three labels a receipt, a pack, an export and a redaction sign with, and a fifth content type. One
; deployment key signs all five, and each of the five passes its own checks, so the field that tells a reader
; which claim it is holding has to be answered before anything is read out of the payload. A reader that took
; an inventory for a pack would be folding a chain over entries that name files rather than over receipts that
; carry them, and reporting a walk nobody performed.
Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Protected-Header = {
    1: -8,                            ; alg: EdDSA (Ed25519)
    4: bstr .size 32,                 ; kid: sha256(public_key)
    3: "ashaveri/epoch-inventory"     ; typ
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The document inside the payload, and how a JSON document is read against a CDDL statement.
;
; The blocks below are the layout, member by member, and they are the same members
; `schemas/epoch-inventory-v1.schema.json` declares and the same ones the reader closes over. CDDL writes
; numbers as `uint` and text as `tstr` because it describes a data model, and a JSON document inhabits that
; model; what differs from a receipt or a pack is the encoding the payload travels in, stated above.
;
; Two rules belong to the JSON reading rather than to any one member, and a reader enforces both while it is
; still reading characters, because afterwards there is nothing left to enforce them on.
;
; A member name appears once. `JSON.parse` keeps the last of two values and says nothing, so a payload naming
; `packs` twice would read as whichever of the two lists the parser reached last while the signed bytes carry
; both. The closure rule below already refuses a member this version does not define; this one refuses a
; member defined twice, which is the same loss of the writer's intent arrived at from the other side.
;
; A number is written as an integer. No position of this layout holds a fraction, a duration with a sub-second
; part, or a quantity so large that a reader of this document cannot state it exactly, so `1.0`, `1e2`, `+1`,
; `01` and `-0` are refused as spellings rather than read as the numbers they equal: the parser hands the
; integer and its float imitation over as one value, and by the time a check could ask which the writer
; signed the question has no answer left in the data. That is the rule every other signed container in this
; estate states as "no floating-point number may appear", arrived at through the encoding this one uses.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; The document, closed at every block, and the field order is the order a writer of this format lists them
; in. `packs` is the run and the three blocks after it are what the run adds up to, which is why they follow
; it rather than preceding it: a reader takes the entries first and the summaries second, and the summaries
; are refused when they are not the arithmetic of the entries.
Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Document = {
    v: 1,                                  ; format version
    epoch: tstr .size (1..200),              ; the operator's label for the run: printable text, unpadded
    manifest: Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Deployment,
    packs: [+ Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Pack],
    window: Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Span,
    chain: Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Run-Chain,
    duty: Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Run-Duty,
}

; Which deployment the run belongs to, named by the three members that identify a deployment manifest. An
; inventory saying only that its keys came from a manifest would name no document, and a reviewer holding two
; of them could not tell which one this run was checked against: the keys a run was resolved against are read
; out of that document, and a run of packs sealed across a rotation resolves under more than one of them.
;
; Nothing here carries the manifest's bytes, its digest or a copy of its key list, because a claim about which
; keys are trusted is the reviewer's own designation and `verifyEpochInventory` authenticates no key of
; itself. Naming the document the writer used is the most this container can honestly say.
;
; The two ids carry the bound the document that supplies them carries and no other, which is a floor of one
; byte and no ceiling. A ceiling written here would refuse an inventory whose own writer had copied a published
; manifest faithfully, and a format that describes an artifact already in the field has no standing to narrow
; the document it names: a narrowing of a copied position arrives as a version of this format, with cases of
; its own, or it does not arrive. The same reason bounds `epoch` and nothing else, because that label is
; written by this container and printed beside the run, so its width is this format's own business.
Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Deployment = {
    iss: tstr,       ; the deployment id, as that manifest states it: never empty, bounded by no ceiling
    ins: tstr,       ; the instance id, as that manifest states it: never empty, bounded by no ceiling
    epk: uint,       ; the key epoch number, as that manifest states it
}

; One pack of the run, stated as a location and the figures read out of the pack that sits there.
;
; `file` and `retention` are relative to the epoch directory and written with forward slashes wherever the run
; is read, and the directory each one names is the pack's own digest: `packs/<digest>/pack-v1.cbor` and
; `packs/<digest>/retention-v1.json`. A closed window is the pair, and an inventory that named only the pack
; would leave a reader unable to tell which retention artifact the duty figures were read with.
;
; The two digests are sha256 over whole files as they sit on the volume: `sha256` of the sealed pack document,
; tag and signature included, which is the identity a redaction of that pack names it by, and
; `retentionSha256` of the retention artifact beside it. Both are recomputable by a reader holding the files,
; and this document carries no claim about whether they are the files a reviewer is handed, because a reader
; of this document alone was not handed them. See the note at the foot of this file.
;
; `at`, `span`, `items`, the two `chain` endpoints, `kid` and the four `duty` integers are the pack's own
; figures, copied after the pack was read through `verifyPack`, and not re-derived here. A conclusion is not
; among them: `duty` carries the two integers a pack signs and never a word about whether the duty was met,
; because whether it was owed at all turns on the mapping the revision names and on the law behind it.
Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Pack = {
    file: tstr,                    ; packs/<sha256>/pack-v1.cbor
    retention: tstr,               ; packs/<sha256>/retention-v1.json
    sha256: tstr,                  ; 64 lowercase hex characters
    retentionSha256: tstr,         ; 64 lowercase hex characters
    at: uint,                      ; unix seconds, the instant that pack began to be assembled
    span: Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Span,
    items: uint,                   ; the receipts that pack carries
    chain: Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Pack-Chain,
    kid: tstr,                     ; 64 lowercase hex characters, the key that sealed that pack
    duty: Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Pack-Duty,
}

; A half-open window, from included and to excluded, which is the pack's own framing and this document's
; copying of it. A receipt stamped exactly at `to` belongs to the next window, which is what lets the windows
; of a run meet without overlapping.
Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Span = {
    from: uint,
    to: uint,
}

; The two endpoints the pack signs, as hex. `anchor` is what the pack's first record was chained from and
; `head` the digest of its last, so the pair is what a reader compares between neighbours below.
Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Pack-Chain = {
    anchor: tstr,                  ; 64 lowercase hex characters
    head: tstr,                    ; 64 lowercase hex characters
}

; The four integers a pack signs, as signed. `art` is a label from the retention-duty registry, which this
; format declares a `tstr` rather than an enum for the reason `pack.cddl` gives: the registry is not this
; estate's to interpret. It is declared at the width `pack.cddl` declares it, which is no width, and read the
; way that file's reader reads it, as a string that is not empty: an inventory copies the label a pack signed,
; and a ceiling written here would refuse a pack the pack format seals.
Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Pack-Duty = {
    art: tstr,                       ; a label from the retention-duty registry, e.g. "19(1)": never empty
    rev: uint,                     ; unix seconds, the revision `required` was read from
    required: uint,                ; seconds that revision required
    held: uint,                    ; seconds that store had held its oldest retained receipt, at `at`
}

; The run's chain, which is the second thing an inventory exists to state after its window.
;
; `anchor` is the first pack's anchor and `head` the last pack's head, where first and last are fixed by the
; windows and not by the order the entries were written in. `continuous` says whether each pack's anchor is the
; head before it, and `breaks` states each pair where it is not, in run order, with the two digests that
; disagree beside it. A break is reported and not explained away: the two digests are the evidence, and what
; happened between them is a fact about a deployment's store rather than about this document.
;
; The two are read together and neither is decorative. A `continuous` of true beside an empty `breaks` is a
; claim that this run chains from its anchor to its head, and a reader recomputes it from the entries; a
; document that smoothed a break into `true` would be stating a continuity its own figures contradict, which
; is the defect the pair exists to make visible. A `false` beside an empty list is the same disagreement
; arrived at from the other side, and it is refused the same way.
Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Run-Chain = {
    anchor: tstr,                  ; 64 lowercase hex characters, the first pack's anchor
    head: tstr,                    ; 64 lowercase hex characters, the last pack's head
    continuous: bool,
    breaks: [* Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Break],
}

; One place where the run does not continue itself, named by the pack that failed to carry the head before it
; forward and by the two digests that show it. `file` names a pack of this run: a row about a pack the
; document does not describe is a statement about something else, and it is refused rather than dropped.
Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Break = {
    file: tstr,                    ; one of this document's `file` values
    afterHead: tstr,               ; the head the predecessor pack states
    anchor: tstr,                  ; the anchor this pack carries instead
}

; The duty the sealed run carries, stated per pack and nowhere as a verdict.
;
; `carried` is the emptiness of `short` and nothing else, and each row of `short` is one pack whose own
; `held` falls short of its own `required`, with `shortBy` the subtraction between them. Each pack is measured
; against its own two integers and never against a neighbour's, so a routed article's period is not read
; against another article's figure: a run whose declarations differ reports each on its own terms. A shortfall
; is lawful output, and whether it was owed is settled outside this format.
Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Run-Duty = {
    carried: bool,
    short: [* Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Shortfall],
}

; One unmet pack, restating the four figures the arithmetic is made of beside the seconds it fell short by, so
; a reader can see the subtraction without going back to the entry.
Ashaveri-Epoch-Inventory-Shortfall = {
    file: tstr,                    ; one of this document's `file` values
    art: tstr,                     ; that pack's own duty label, at the width the pack states it
    required: uint,                ; that pack's own required seconds
    held: uint,                    ; that pack's own held seconds
    shortBy: uint,                 ; required minus held, which is never zero
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Closure is one rule across this file and not a rule about one map, as `receipt.cddl`, `pack.cddl` and
; `redaction.cddl` state it. No block above carries a `...`, and the absence means here what it means there: a
; member the version named by `v` does not define makes the document invalid rather than a document read with
; the unexpected member dropped. That reaches every block of the document and the signed header alike, and the
; header closes under the stronger reason: its bytes are hashed into the `Sig_structure`, so a label this block
; does not name is an authenticated parameter, and a reader that took the three it knows and returned those
; would hand its caller a document other than the one that was signed. The header's members are the COSE
; registry's integers, which is why they are numbered rather than named.
;
; One map a signer fills at will, and that is a decision rather than a gap: the `unprotected` map of
; `COSE_Sign1-Epoch-Inventory-COSE` sits outside the signature, so nothing written in it can travel as a claim
; about the run, and enforcing that it is empty would buy strictness with no security content behind it. What
; the format declares of it is only that it carries no claim.
;
; Sign is the rule that a bare `uint` does not carry: every instant in this document is a unix time no earlier
; than the epoch and every count a whole number no smaller than zero, so a negative figure is a malformed
; document rather than an unusual way of writing a quantity. CDDL writes `uint` because it owns no clock, and
; the bound that matters is the one the encoding enforces above, that a number is written as an integer at all.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What the run states about itself, which is where this container differs from every other signed document in
; this estate. A pack's items are ordered by their links and their endpoints are inside the signature, so a
; reader walks them. An inventory's entries are ordered by their windows, and the order is derived by a reader
; rather than claimed by an array position: position in `packs` bears nothing, exactly as position bears
; nothing in a pack's items. Three statements follow from the entries and are restated inside the signature,
; and a reader recomputes all three and refuses a document whose restatement is not its own arithmetic.
;
; The windows meet. Each entry's `span` runs forwards, and each entry's `span.from` is its predecessor's
; `span.to`. A gap is a period the epoch states it attests and does not, and an overlap seals the same
; receipts twice under two signatures while reporting them as one run. `window` is then the outer edges of the
; windows the packs state, and nothing else: it is not the period somebody asked for, and a reader that cared
; about that compared it with the request, which no reading of this document can see.
;
; The chain continues, pair by pair, which is what gives the two endpoints their meaning: a run that chains
; from its anchor to its head is a sequence of packs a reader can lay end to end, and a run that does not says
; so with the two digests of each place it does not.
;
; The duty adds up, which is `carried` and the `short` rows above.
;
; The arrays of the document have no floor except `packs`, which `[+ ...]` requires to hold at least one entry.
; A gap between two windows is refused, so a run of one pack states a window and a run of ten states the span
; the ten cover; an inventory of no packs states neither, and states a chain and a duty about nothing, which is
; why it is refused as a malformed document rather than accepted as an honest report of an empty epoch. The
; honest report of a period that sealed no receipts is the artifact that states windows and retention, which is
; a pack's pair and not this document.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; What a verified inventory does not establish, stated here because a reimplementer who has just read the word
; `verify` is most likely to over-read exactly this.
;
; It is not a claim that the packs exist, or that they say what this document says they say. The reader is
; handed this document and nothing else, and the digests, item counts and per-pack figures in it are the
; writer's restatement of packs it was never given. A reviewer who wants that check runs `verifyPack` over the
; files the entries name and compares each entry's `sha256` with `redactionPackDigest` of the bytes it holds,
; which is the second check a reader makes and not a conclusion of this one. That is why the reader is handed a
; document rather than pointed at a directory: an inventory is the artifact that travels, and a verdict about
; it has to be reachable by somebody with no access to the volume it was written on.
;
; It is not a claim that the run is complete. An inventory lists the packs its directory held when it was
; written, and a directory with a window left out of it reads exactly like an epoch that sealed no more. This
; is the same bound a pack's head puts on a walk: a deletion is visible only against a copy the reader had
; cause to believe before, and this container states its own figures and no reader's expectation.
;
; It is not a statement about a duty, a lawful basis or a request. `duty.short` is arithmetic on two integers
; each pack signed and `states` is nowhere in this format: which period was owed, to whom, and under which
; mapping revision, is settled outside this document by whoever holds the mapping and the law.
;
; It is not a claim about freshness, and it is not a claim about the deployment manifest it names. The three
; figures of `manifest` say which document the writer resolved keys against; whether that document is the
; deployment's own, and whether the keys listed in it are trusted for anything, are answered by reading it,
; which this container neither does nor replaces.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
